WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.476
Welcome to domain three of the Network Plus N10-009 exam: network operations.

00:00:05.476 --> 00:00:08.392
It is worth nineteen percent of the exam.

00:00:08.392 --> 00:00:19.772
We will cover documentation and change management, monitoring, disaster recovery, network services such as D H C P, D N S, and N T P, and remote access methods.

00:00:20.472 --> 00:00:22.032
Start with documentation.

00:00:22.032 --> 00:00:25.402
Network diagrams show the physical and logical layout.

00:00:25.402 --> 00:00:28.335
Rack diagrams show what sits in each rack unit.

00:00:28.335 --> 00:00:31.393
Cable maps show which cable connects which ports.

00:00:31.393 --> 00:00:34.201
I P A M tracks address space and assignments.

00:00:34.201 --> 00:00:38.320
A wireless survey and heat map shows coverage and signal strength.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:42.065
And an asset inventory tracks devices, owners, and licenses.

00:00:42.065 --> 00:00:45.747
Good documentation also helps you rebuild after a disaster.

00:00:46.447 --> 00:00:56.377
For life cycle, end of life means the vendor stops selling a product, while end of support means the vendor stops patches and help, which is when security gaps appear.

00:00:56.377 --> 00:01:02.978
Decommissioning means backing up needed data, wiping the device, and removing it from inventory and monitoring.

00:01:02.978 --> 00:01:13.206
Change management is the formal process: request, assess, get approval, schedule a maintenance window, back up first, have a rollback plan, implement, verify, and document.

00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:16.060
The production configuration is what is running.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:22.066
The golden configuration is the tested, approved baseline used as the reference for integrity checks.

00:01:22.766 --> 00:01:25.396
Objective three point two is monitoring.

00:01:25.396 --> 00:01:26.843
S N M P polls devices.

00:01:26.843 --> 00:01:33.615
An agent on the device answers a manager, the data is organized in a M I B, and each item has an O I D.

00:01:33.615 --> 00:01:36.771
Traps are alerts that devices send on their own.

00:01:36.771 --> 00:01:40.519
S N M P version three adds authentication and encryption.

00:01:40.519 --> 00:01:45.713
Flow data, such as NetFlow and I P F I X, shows who talks to whom and how much.

00:01:45.713 --> 00:01:47.751
Syslog collects logs centrally.

00:01:47.751 --> 00:01:50.644
A S I E M correlates logs and raises alerts.

00:01:50.644 --> 00:01:54.984
And a baseline records normal behavior so you can spot deviations.

00:01:55.684 --> 00:01:58.943
Monitor interface utilization, errors, and discards.

00:01:58.943 --> 00:02:02.703
Watch device health, such as C P U, memory, and temperature.

00:02:02.703 --> 00:02:04.583
Track availability and uptime.

00:02:04.583 --> 00:02:08.093
Use environmental sensors for heat, humidity, and water.

00:02:08.093 --> 00:02:11.916
Watch for configuration changes and analyze traffic patterns.

00:02:11.916 --> 00:02:15.676
And set alerts at thresholds that are tied to your baseline.

00:02:16.376 --> 00:02:17.474
Disaster recovery.

00:02:17.474 --> 00:02:22.965
R P O is the recovery point objective, how much data loss is acceptable, measured in time.

00:02:22.965 --> 00:02:26.930
R T O is the recovery time objective, how long recovery can take.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:31.932
M T T R is the mean time to repair, and M T B F is the mean time between failures.

00:02:31.932 --> 00:02:35.410
A cold site is just space and is the slowest to bring up.

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:39.131
A warm site has equipment ready and needs some data restored.

00:02:39.131 --> 00:02:43.462
A hot site is a fully running copy, the fastest and the most expensive.

00:02:44.162 --> 00:02:52.069
For redundancy, active-active means all nodes carry traffic, and active-passive means a standby takes over when the primary fails.

00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:55.657
Know the backup types: full, incremental, and differential.

00:02:55.657 --> 00:03:00.705
The three-two-one rule means three copies, on two types of media, with one offsite.

00:03:00.705 --> 00:03:07.881
A tabletop exercise walks the team through a scenario on paper, and validation tests prove that the plan really works.

00:03:08.581 --> 00:03:10.001
Now network services.

00:03:10.001 --> 00:03:14.731
D H C P follows four steps: discover, offer, request, and acknowledge.

00:03:14.731 --> 00:03:20.948
Know the scope, the reservation, which gives a device a fixed address by mac, and the lease.

00:03:20.948 --> 00:03:24.259
A D H C P relay forwards requests across subnets.

00:03:24.259 --> 00:03:30.881
In D N S, an A record maps a name to an I P v four address, and a quad A record maps to I P v six.

00:03:30.881 --> 00:03:34.530
A C name is an alias, and a P T R is a reverse lookup.

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:41.693
M X names the mail server, N S names the name server, and T X T holds text, often used for email security.

00:03:41.693 --> 00:03:44.329
The hosts file is checked before D N S.

00:03:45.029 --> 00:03:52.314
N T P synchronizes clocks over U D P one twenty-three, using strata to describe distance from the time source.

00:03:52.314 --> 00:03:56.421
P T P is far more precise and is used on specialized networks.

00:03:56.421 --> 00:03:59.534
N T S is a secure, authenticated form of N T P.

00:03:59.534 --> 00:04:03.640
With I P v six, hosts can build their own address using slack.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:10.396
Encrypted D N S comes as D o H and D o T, and D N S sec signs D N S answers to prove they are genuine.

00:04:11.096 --> 00:04:12.783
Last, access and management.

00:04:12.783 --> 00:04:19.649
S S H gives an encrypted command line on port twenty-two and replaces Telnet, which sends everything in cleartext.

00:04:19.649 --> 00:04:25.130
A console connection or out-of-band network works even when the production network is down.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:28.864
A site-to-site V P N links two networks, usually with I P sec.

00:04:28.864 --> 00:04:31.333
A client-to-site V P N connects one user.

00:04:31.333 --> 00:04:37.417
With split tunneling only corporate traffic uses the V P N, and with full tunneling all traffic does.

00:04:37.417 --> 00:04:44.463
A jump box is a hardened host used to reach internal devices, and R D P and A P I s are other ways to manage systems.

00:04:45.163 --> 00:04:53.084
To recap domain three: know the difference between end of life and end of support, and what a golden configuration is.

00:04:53.084 --> 00:04:56.105
Know S N M P, flow data, syslog, and S I E M.

00:04:56.105 --> 00:05:01.407
Know the difference between R P O and R T O, and the cold, warm, and hot sites.

00:05:01.407 --> 00:05:06.643
Know the D H C P steps, the D N S record types, and N T P on one twenty-three.

00:05:06.643 --> 00:05:09.999
Prefer S S H over Telnet and know the V P N types.

00:05:09.999 --> 00:05:10.671
Good luck.
